physical product (including a physical distribution medium)", so they address
distribution on media; 6(c) is narrower still, permitted only for conveying that
is occasional and noncommercial, and only by someone who themselves received a
6(b) offer. This image is conveyed over a network from a container registry, which
is the situation clause 6(d) describes: "Convey the object code by offering access
from a designated place (gratis or for a charge), and offer equivalent access to
the Corresponding Source in the same way through the same place at no further
charge."

The written offer above is therefore the conservative form, not the clause that
most directly fits how this image is published. Relying on 6(d) instead — pointing
recipients at the upstream and conda-forge sources for the exact pinned versions,
as listed above — is a legitimate alternative, but it is not obligation-free. It
requires that "you maintain clear directions next to the object code saying where
to find the Corresponding Source", and provides that "you remain obligated to
ensure that it is available for as long as needed to satisfy these requirements" —
an open-ended availability duty, in place of the written offer's three-year
contactable one.

Choosing between the two is a matter for the open-source licence review, not a
settled question. Once chosen, this section should be edited to state only the
route actually relied upon; keeping both is ambiguous about which obligation has
been accepted.


